<clickhouse>
    <!--
        Small-host profile (one file per concern; mounted as a single file because the image entrypoint
        writes generated config files into /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/).

        WHY
        Stock ClickHouse is sized for a dedicated server. On a 4-8 GB host that also runs the app, Kafka,
        Postgres and Redis it (1) lets the process grow to 90% of the RAM it sees, (2) keeps 5 GiB of mark
        cache and 8 GiB of uncompressed cache as ceilings, and (3) writes a dozen system log tables whose
        background merges alone kept the memory tracker at its cap: system.trace_log reached 3.7 GiB (266
        million rows) in a week, text_log 460 MiB. The visible symptoms were MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED (code 241)
        in unrelated queries and a Docker VM disk that filled up. This profile bounds all three.

        MEMORY CEILING
        max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio is a fraction of the memory ClickHouse sees: the container
        limit when one is set (docker-compose.yml: mem_limit ${CLICKHOUSE_MEM_LIMIT:-3g}; ClickHouse reads the
        cgroup), the host RAM otherwise. 0.7 of the 3 GiB default is about 2.1 GiB (2.25 GB), which leaves the
        rest of the container limit to allocator overhead, page-cache-backed mmap and the Kafka consumer
        threads, so the server refuses a query with a clean error long before the kernel could OOM-kill it.
        Check the effective value with:
            SELECT value FROM system.server_settings WHERE name = 'max_server_memory_usage'
        Raising CLICKHOUSE_MEM_LIMIT raises the ceiling proportionally (no edit of this file needed).

        CACHES
        mark cache 256 MiB (default 5 GiB), index mark cache 64 MiB, uncompressed cache off (default 8 GiB;
        it only helps repeated small reads of hot data, and the OS page cache serves the same reads). The
        events table is small compared to these caches, so hit rates do not suffer.

        POOLS AND CONCURRENCY
        background_schedule_pool_size 16 (the Kafka engine consumers, the materialized view and the mutation
        machinery use this pool: keep it above the number of Kafka consumers plus a margin).
        max_concurrent_queries 50 (default 100) is above the app's own limits (insight queries per project)
        and stops a runaway client from queueing 100 queries on a small host.

        CRASH TRAPS (each one was observed on 25.8 while validating this file: the server exits with code
        91 or 36 and the container restarts in a loop; check `docker compose logs clickhouse`)
          * Do NOT lower background_pool_size below the default 16 (a value of 4 aborted startup with exit
            code 91 when other pool settings were not lowered with it). Leave it alone.
          * Do NOT give query_log or part_log a custom engine element: it clashes with the default
            partition_by of the table and the server exits with code 36 (BAD_ARGUMENTS). Use the ttl element
            below, nothing else.
          * An element the server does not know is ignored, but a malformed one is fatal: validate any edit
            in a scratch container (same image, a memory limit of 3g, this file mounted into config.d, no network
            route to the live Kafka) before recreating the live one.

        SYSTEM LOG TABLES
        remove="1" switches a log off at startup (nothing is written, nothing is merged). The tables that
        already exist are NOT dropped automatically: drop them once by hand, one at a time, run
        DROP TABLE IF EXISTS system.trace_log SYNC and likewise for every table removed below). They are disposable diagnostics, not user data.
        Kept on purpose: query_log (with a 7-day TTL) because scripts and probes correlate by log_comment, and
        part_log (7-day TTL) because the merge and mutation history of events helps when ingestion misbehaves.
        The app itself reads system.mutations, kafka_consumers, parts, disks, tables, settings, row_policies and
        grants, none of which are log tables.

        HOW TO APPLY
        This is a SINGLE-FILE bind mount, which follows the inode. Editing the file in place (sed -i, most
        editors) or `git pull` / checkout (unlink + create) is NOT picked up by `docker compose up -d`. Run:
            docker compose up -d - -force-recreate clickhouse
        (read '- -force-recreate' as two hyphens followed by force-recreate: XML comments cannot contain two
        consecutive hyphens). Existing installs need this once, because the compose file gained new mounts.
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>0.7</max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>

    <mark_cache_size>268435456</mark_cache_size>
    <index_mark_cache_size>67108864</index_mark_cache_size>
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>

    <background_schedule_pool_size>16</background_schedule_pool_size>
    <max_concurrent_queries>50</max_concurrent_queries>

    <trace_log remove="1"/>
    <text_log remove="1"/>
    <metric_log remove="1"/>
    <asynchronous_metric_log remove="1"/>
    <processors_profile_log remove="1"/>
    <query_thread_log remove="1"/>
    <query_views_log remove="1"/>
    <query_metric_log remove="1"/>
    <error_log remove="1"/>
    <opentelemetry_span_log remove="1"/>
    <zookeeper_log remove="1"/>
    <session_log remove="1"/>
    <crash_log remove="1"/>

    <query_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_log>
    <part_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </part_log>
</clickhouse>
